metric_pages = [
{'heading':'Primary outcome metric definitions','items':[
 ('p','The catalogue defines standard starting measures. Select diagnostics that can change a decision. Unless stated otherwise, calculate per application for each complete sprint and the agreed rolling evaluation window. Record the metric version, sample size, exclusions, and evidence cutoff in T02.'),
 ('m','M01','Time to accepted production value',
  'For each eligible work item, measure calendar days from recorded commitment to the later of production availability and business acceptance. Report median and 85th percentile. Also report commitment to availability and availability to acceptance separately.',
  'Work tracker, deployment records, and product acceptance record. Owner: delivery lead with product owner. Report completed items and unfinished item age using M11.',
  'Lower is generally better for comparable work. Keep acceptance rules stable. Administrative acceptance delays must remain visible. Commit-to-production lead time is separately defined as M04.'),
 ('m','M02','Delivery effort and cost per outcome',
  'Sum attributable person-hours for development, review, QA, security, documentation, operations, and corrective work. Divide by comparable accepted production outcomes. Report hours per outcome. Cost per outcome uses documented loaded rates plus allocated non-labor delivery costs.',
  'Task effort records or a disclosed sample, payroll or finance rate assumptions, and delivery IDs. Owner: engineering lead with finance. Reconcile work cohorts and follow-up periods.',
  'Lower is better when scope and quality remain comparable. Include effort spent on abandoned work in period totals. Separate one-time adoption cost. Do not treat elapsed time reductions as equivalent labor savings.'),
 ('m','M03','Realized net adoption value',
  'Over a stated period and cost basis, subtract incremental adoption costs from verified incremental financial benefits. Report currency, gross benefit, total cost, net value, and realized ROI. Show cumulative results including transition cost.',
  'T11 benefit and cost ledger, financial evidence, business outcome records, and named verification. Owner: finance or delegated benefit owner.',
  'Positive value supports investment when risk limits pass. Recovered capacity and modeled risk reduction remain separate until realized. Negative or unavailable value is reported directly. Calculation rules and examples follow the catalogue.')
]},
{'heading':'Production delivery metric definitions','items':[
 ('p','M04-M08 use the five DORA metric concepts [S3]. The cohort and counting rules below are local operational definitions. Freeze them before comparison. Use production deployments of a named service; remove non-production runs and duplicate pipeline events.'),
 ('m','M04','Change lead time',
  'For each included commit, elapsed hours from version-control commit timestamp to its first successful production deployment. Report median and 85th percentile. Document handling of squash commits, cherry-picks, and migrated history.',
  'Version control and deployment manifest. Owner: delivery platform owner. Keep source-to-artifact links and excluded or unmatched commit counts.',
  'Lower can indicate faster delivery. Report unshipped changes separately. Large and small commits differ, so inspect work mix and review burden. This begins later than the commitment-based M01.'),
 ('m','M05','Deployment frequency',
  'Count distinct deployments that make a production service version available during the window, divided by calendar days or weeks in that window. State the unit. Count one logical rollout once across its replicas.',
  'Production release records and artifact IDs. Owner: release or platform owner. State treatment of configuration-only changes and rollback deployments.',
  'More frequent delivery can be useful, but deployment count is not customer value. Pair with stability and acceptance. Do not require an arbitrary deployment target for every service.'),
 ('m','M06','Failed deployment recovery time',
  'Elapsed hours from the failed production deployment or its failure onset, using a fixed documented convention, until recovery is verified. Report median, 85th percentile, count, and unresolved failures. Prefer the actual failure onset when known.',
  'Deployment and incident timelines with service recovery evidence. Owner: operations lead. Retain the selected start convention and missing timestamps.',
  'Lower is better. Report no observed failed deployments as no sample, not zero-hour recovery. This measure covers deployment failures, rather than all incident recovery.')
]},
{'heading':'Production stability and observation rules','items':[
 ('m','M07','Change fail rate',
  'Distinct production deployments that cause a failure requiring immediate intervention divided by all included production deployments, multiplied by 100. Retain the failure severity and parent deployment link.',
  'Release and incident records. Owner: operations with QA. Use a declared failure attribution window and consistent operational criteria.',
  'Lower is better. Report numerator and denominator. One deployment counts once even if it causes multiple incidents. Also inspect severe incidents independently of the rate.'),
 ('m','M08','Deployment rework rate',
  'Unplanned production deployments performed to correct production defects or incidents divided by all included production deployments, multiplied by 100. Apply a fixed classification at review.',
  'Release purpose, incident or defect references, and deployment ID. Owner: release lead. Distinguish corrective deployments from planned enhancements.',
  'Lower is generally better. A corrective deployment may itself fail, so M07 and M08 overlap and must not be added. Rework outside production deployment is captured in M13.'),
 ('h','Apply the same follow up'),
 ('p','Assign failures to their originating deployment cohort even when detected after sprint close. State the follow-up window and publish revisions when late evidence arrives. Keep recent cohorts provisional. A separate operational incident view may report incidents by detection date, but it must not be substituted for the deployment cohort rate.'),
 ('h','Example of aggregation'),
 ('p','If one sprint has one failed deployment out of ten and the next has one out of two, the combined rate is two out of twelve, or 16.7 percent. Averaging 10 percent and 50 percent would produce an incorrect 30 percent combined rate.'),
 ('p','The five delivery metrics are interpreted together for a service. They support improvement discussions and should not be blended into a universal ranking of unrelated teams [S3].')
]},
{'heading':'Workflow diagnostic metric definitions','items':[
 ('m','M09','Review waiting time',
  'For each review cycle, elapsed hours from ready-for-review to the first substantive human response. Sum waiting intervals per PR or report cycle distributions consistently. Exclude explicitly marked author draft time.',
  'PR state and review events. Owner: engineering lead. Record bot responses separately and retain draft transitions.',
  'Lower is useful if review quality holds. A quick acknowledgement alone is not substantive review. Check whether AI adoption increases the number or complexity of review cycles.'),
 ('m','M10','Blocked work time',
  'Hours in explicitly recorded blocked status divided by hours from work start to completion or current cutoff, multiplied by 100. Use the same elapsed or business-hours basis for both terms.',
  'Work tracker transitions and blocker categories. Owner: delivery lead. Validate consistent use of blocked status.',
  'Lower is better when reporting behavior is stable. Break down environment, dependency, knowledge, and approval blockers. Missing blocker tagging limits the result.'),
 ('m','M11','Unfinished work age and carryover',
  'For open committed items, report count and median and 85th-percentile age from commitment to cutoff. Carryover is unfinished items from the original sprint commitment divided by items committed at sprint start.',
  'Sprint snapshots and current work status. Owner: delivery lead. Report added, removed, and canceled items separately.',
  'Lower age and carryover can indicate healthier flow. They also depend on work size and priority changes. Keep original commitments intact rather than rewriting the denominator.'),
 ('m','M12','Build and test feedback time',
  'Elapsed minutes from an eligible CI run being queued to actionable build and test results. Report median, 85th percentile, queue time, and run time. Separate PR validation from release pipelines.',
  'CI timestamps and run categories. Owner: platform or QA lead. Include failed and canceled runs in their appropriate categories.',
  'Lower is better if checks remain effective. Track flaky tests and bypassed checks. Do not obtain a faster result by silently removing required validation.')
]},
{'heading':'Quality and security metric definitions','items':[
 ('m','M13','Corrective effort share',
  'Person-hours correcting defects in previously delivered work divided by total recorded delivery person-hours in the same period, multiplied by 100. Split pre-production and production correction and avoid duplicate time entries.',
  'Effort records linked to defect IDs. Owner: QA with engineering. Retain original delivery cohort where identifiable.',
  'Lower is better under stable detection. Report sampled coverage. Keep AI output correction identifiable so review or test effort is not mistaken for a net saving.'),
 ('m','M14','Escaped defects',
  'Confirmed production defects detected within the declared follow-up per 100 delivered outcomes, grouped by severity. Also report absolute severe-defect counts. Use equal follow-up across cohorts.',
  'Defect and incident records linked to delivered outcomes. Owner: QA. De-duplicate related reports and document severity changes.',
  'Lower is better. More testing or reporting can increase detected defects without worsening code. Pair with test coverage and customer impact. Recent releases remain provisional.'),
 ('m','M15','Vulnerability remediation and exposure',
  'Report open vulnerability count, median and 85th-percentile age, and overdue count by severity at cutoff. For resolved findings, report time from validated detection to verified remediation. Keep accepted exceptions separate.',
  'Security finding system, asset inventory, and remediation evidence. Owner: security lead. Use stable finding identities across repeated scans.',
  'Lower exposure and faster remediation are desirable. Never average away a critical finding. Report changes in scan coverage. A waived issue is not a remediated issue.'),
 ('m','M16','Required control execution',
  'Eligible changes with all applicable required control results present divided by eligible changes, multiplied by 100. Report failures, bypasses, and approved exceptions separately from execution.',
  'CI policy results, security checks, and release approvals. Owner: security with platform. Freeze the applicability rules.',
  'Higher execution is desirable, but execution alone does not establish effectiveness. Missing results are incomplete evidence. Use SSDF to organize local secure development expectations [S9].')
]},
{'heading':'Experience and adoption metric definitions','items':[
 ('m','M17','Developer experience pulse',
  'Report the distribution and median for each stable five-point feedback question, plus favorable responses of four or five divided by valid responses. Report response count and eligible count; exclude not-applicable answers.',
  'T06-T08 surveys. Owner: engineering enablement or research owner. Protect respondent identity and retain question versions.',
  'Use individual questions for toil, cognitive burden, review, and collaboration. Do not conceal a worsening item in a composite average. Perceived time savings need independent validation.'),
 ('m','M18','AI exposure and adoption',
  'Work exposure is eligible work items with documented AI use divided by all eligible items. Separately report unknown exposure share, access allocation, and active eligible users under a declared usage definition.',
  'Approved tool metadata or structured work tags. Owner: enablement lead. Record assignment to rollout groups separately from actual use.',
  'Adoption is a diagnostic, not proof of value. Changes in eligibility alter the denominator. Do not compare voluntary users with nonusers as if assignment were random.'),
 ('m','M19','AI output evaluation results',
  'Reviewed AI-assisted outputs meeting the declared acceptance rubric divided by reviewed AI-assisted outputs. Report first-review results and final results separately, with rejection and correction reasons.',
  'Structured review sample and task IDs. Owner: engineering or QA. Record sampling method, sample count, rubric, and evaluator.',
  'A higher pass rate may indicate suitability, but easy samples can inflate it. IDE suggestion acceptance is a different vendor measure. Generated tests must be checked for meaningful assertions.'),
 ('m','M20','Net task effort change',
  'Compare total person-hours per comparable task before and after the change, including prompting, verification, review, tests, correction, and documentation. Report absolute hours and relative change.',
  'A declared effort sample or reliable task records. Owner: engineering with measurement owner. Record work class and number sampled.',
  'A positive saving can support a capacity estimate. Do not multiply an unrepresentative sample across all engineering work. Keep one-time training in the investment ledger.')
]},
{'heading':'Business and documentation metric definitions','items':[
 ('m','M21','Sprint Goal and acceptance results',
  'Record Sprint Goal achieved yes, partial, or no with product-owner rationale. Separately report accepted delivered outcomes divided by outcomes submitted for acceptance, using a declared follow-up window.',
  'Sprint Goal record and acceptance decisions. Owner: product owner. Retain scope changes and acceptance reasons.',
  'Interpret the goal record qualitatively and report acceptance numerators. Avoid inventing a numerical value for partial achievement. Ticket count alone does not establish business value.'),
 ('m','M22','Customer or business outcome',
  'Choose one outcome tied to the initiative, such as successful eligible customer tasks divided by attempted tasks, adoption among eligible customers, or support cases per 100 active accounts. Freeze the event and denominator definitions.',
  'Product analytics, support records, or the authoritative business system. Owner: product or business owner. Use T02 to specify the chosen outcome and attribution method.',
  'Direction depends on the outcome. Segment by relevant customer groups and account for release exposure and seasonality. Customer-visible impact may lag sprint completion.'),
 ('m','M23','Verified documentation coverage',
  'Required information items that are present, owned, version-linked, and reviewed within their local validity period divided by all applicable required information items, multiplied by 100.',
  'Documentation inventory with evidence links and review dates. Owner: service documentation owner. Approve and explain not-applicable exclusions.',
  'Higher coverage supports readiness. Coverage does not prove correctness. Validate representative content, including requirements, architecture, tests, threats, and recovery guidance.'),
 ('m','M24','Documentation task success',
  'Sampled knowledge tasks completed correctly using maintained documentation within the declared limit divided by attempted sampled tasks. Report time distribution and task class as well as pass rate.',
  'Onboarding, support, or runbook exercises. Owner: documentation with operations. Preserve task scripts and evaluator notes.',
  'Higher success and shorter comparable task times indicate usefulness. Examples include locating a design decision or following a recovery procedure. Keep test tasks representative.')
]},
{'heading':'AI risk and operational metric definitions','items':[
 ('p','These are proposed measures for the local AI use case, informed by NIST AI RMF and its generative AI profile [S6-S8]. Their thresholds are organizational choices. They are not official NIST adoption scores.'),
 ('m','M25','AI evaluation pass rate',
  'Test cases meeting their predefined criteria divided by valid executed cases, by task and risk category. Report critical failures individually, excluded cases, repeated trials, and unresolved failures.',
  'Versioned evaluation set, model and tool configuration, permissions, and evaluator. Owner: AI risk with QA. Separate development cases from held-out cases.',
  'Higher can support a decision only for tested conditions. Evaluate unreliable output, data handling, malicious context, and unsafe actions where applicable. Re-test relevant cases after material changes.'),
 ('m','M26','Human oversight coverage',
  'AI-assisted changes or actions requiring human approval with evidenced approval before execution divided by all AI-assisted changes or actions requiring approval. Report unapproved execution count separately.',
  'PR approvals, release controls, agent action logs, and exposure records. Owner: engineering with AI risk. Scope depends on privilege and consequence.',
  'Required oversight should be complete. Approval presence does not establish adequate review. Sample reviewer understanding and test evidence. Unknown AI exposure limits coverage claims.'),
 ('m','M27','AI incidents and near misses',
  'Count confirmed incidents and credible near misses by severity, use case, and cause during the period. Track time to containment and unresolved items. Use an exposure-normalized rate only when the exposure denominator is reliable.',
  'Incident system and reporting channel. Owner: security or AI incident lead. Separate confirmed AI contribution from suspected contribution.',
  'Any material harm triggers review under local policy. Zero reports do not prove zero risk. Keep reporting willingness and detection coverage visible.'),
 ('m','M28','AI availability and unit cost',
  'Report successful approved tool requests divided by valid requests, latency percentiles, and incremental tool cost per comparable accepted outcome. Include subscriptions, inference, platform, and support allocations.',
  'Approved provider usage, invoices, and platform telemetry. Owner: platform with finance. Record retries, failures, and shared-cost allocation.',
  'Interpret reliability and cost with delivered quality. Token counts explain consumption but do not measure value. Test the fallback when the provider is unavailable or behavior changes.')
]}
]
